+# Work Ontology & Release Phasing — Design
+
+**Date:** 2026-07-10
+**Status:** PROPOSED — awaiting founder review.
+**Supersedes:** the "modes at the Project level" clause of
+[2026-07-10-vision-and-architecture.md](../plans/2026-07-10-vision-and-architecture.md) §6. All other
+clauses of that document stand.
+
+**Evidence base** (all URL-cited, all with explicit "could not verify" sections):
+- [Open-source Linear alternatives — license audit](#appendix-a-license-audit) (below)
+- [`docs/research/2026-07-10-nesting-depth-evidence.md`](../research/2026-07-10-nesting-depth-evidence.md)
+- [`docs/research/2026-07-10-linear-huly-plane-teardown.md`](../research/2026-07-10-linear-huly-plane-teardown.md)
+- [`docs/research/2026-07-10-meeting-web-capture-and-stt.md`](../research/2026-07-10-meeting-web-capture-and-stt.md)
+
+---
+
+## 1. The decision that started this
+
+**Do we build on top of an open-source Linear alternative?** No. Copy the ontology, not the code.
+
+Two independent reasons, either sufficient:
+
+1. **Licensing.** Every actively-maintained candidate is copyleft. Plane, Vikunja, Kan and Leantime
+   are AGPL-3.0 — the network clause forces source disclosure for anything served over HTTP. Huly is
+   EPL-2.0 (file-level). OpenProject is GPL-3.0 *and* demands a CLA. Operately ships a non-standard
+   "Other" license. Focalboard is AGPL-or-commercial and unmaintained; Tegon is AGPL and archived;
+   Taiga is MPL but dead since Dec 2023. The only fully permissive candidate, Kaneo (MIT), is a plain
+   kanban board weaker than what we already have.
+2. **Stack.** Plane is Django, OpenProject is Rails, Huly is a custom Rust transactor, Focalboard is
+   Go. We are Hono-on-Workers + Neon + Clerk + a React SPA. Forking means abandoning our stack, our
+   design system, and the shipped Workboard to maintain someone else's monolith. Embedding means two
+   auth systems and split-brain data.
+
+Concepts are not copyrightable. Code is. We take the object model and the architectural reasoning,
+and we read docs and engineering write-ups — never source.
+
+---
+
+## 2. The ontology
+
+### 2.1 What was wrong
+
+`work_items` carried **two competing grouping axes**: `department TEXT NOT NULL` and
+`project_id UUID NULL`. Every item was filed by department but only *maybe* by project. Neither axis
+was authoritative, so "where does this go?" had no answer. That — not the absence of a hierarchy —
+is the source of the confusion.
+
+### 2.2 The shape
+
+Linear's docs settle who owns what. Workflow states are configured **per team**, and two teams may
+have different ones. Cycles belong to teams and toggle on/off per team. Triage is a team inbox. Every
+issue belongs to exactly one team; **project is optional** on an issue. A project may span multiple
+teams, carries its own status/health/milestones, and owns no workflow. Initiatives group *projects*,
+not issues.
+
+```
+Tenant (= Clerk Org = Workspace — one level, unchanged)
+  └── Team ............ MANDATORY owner. Carries the mode. Owns workflow, cycles, triage, key.
+        └── Work item .... the atom. status, assignee, priority, type.
+              ├── Sub-item ...... a FULL work item with a parent_id.
+              └── Checklist item  frozen: title, status, due_date.
+
+  └── Project ......... OPTIONAL, cross-team OUTCOME container.
+                        status, lead, target date, milestones. Owns NO workflow.
+        └── (Initiative — deferred — groups projects, not items.)
+```
+
+### 2.3 Naming, and why not "connected tasks"
+
+We already ship `work_item_dependencies` with `blocks` / `depends_on` / `relates_to`. Those *are*
+connections: a DAG, many-to-many, no ownership. A parent/child edge is a different animal: a tree,
+exactly one parent, cascading delete, progress rollup. Naming the tree "connected" hands one word to
+both relationships and destroys the distinction users most need — *this blocks that* versus *this is
+part of that*.
+
+Vikunja is the cautionary case: it models parent/child as just another relation kind, and so "there
+is no single-parent constraint to violate; the DB cannot stop a cycle."
+
+| Layer | Name | Definition |
+|---|---|---|
+| Container | **Project** | Cross-team outcome. Owns milestones, not workflow. |
+| Partition | **Team** | Mandatory owner. Carries the mode. |
+| Unit | **Item** | The atom: status, assignee, priority, type. |
+| Child | **Sub-item** | A full Item that has a parent. Rare; see §2.6. |
+| Checklist | **Step** | A row in an Item's *Checklist*. Frozen at title, status, due date. |
+
+**Rejected: `Task` as the top-level word.** Renaming `work item` → *Task* (with *Items* beneath it)
+was considered and rejected twice over. First, an "Item" nested inside a "Task" sounds *smaller than
+its parent* — the same inversion bug, relocated. Second, and decisively: **the schema's `tasks` table
+IS the checklist.** A UI where "Task" means `work_items` while `tasks` means checklist rows is a
+permanent trap, and a specifically dangerous one here — `work_items.source` has an `agent` value, so
+an agent instructed to "create a task" writes the wrong table.
+
+**Item** is "work item" minus "work": the UI label stays adjacent to the schema name. It is also
+monday.com's atom at scale. Checklist rows are **Steps**, under a section headed **Checklist** — which
+dissolves the "item appears twice" collision.
+
+**Labels only. No rename in schema or contracts.** `work_item` stays in the schema, `WorkItem` stays
+in `packages/contracts`. Renaming the contract type is a **major semver break and Forge depends on
+it**. Label map, to be carried into the canonical doc:
+
+| UI label | Backing |
+|---|---|
+| Item | `work_items` |
+| Sub-item | `work_items` with `parent_id` |
+| Step | `tasks` |
+| Checklist | the `tasks` collection under one Item |
+
+### 2.4 Decisions
+
+| # | Decision | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|
+| O1 | `teams` table; `work_items.team_id NOT NULL` | `department` was Linear's Team, badly typed. Team is the mandatory owner. Departments map 1:1 (founder-confirmed). |
+| O2 | **Modes live on Team**, not Project | §6 amendment. See §3. |
+| O3 | `project_id` stays NULLABLE | With modes on Team, unprojected items are governed by their team. **No fake Inbox projects** — they pollute the project list and corrupt import semantics, since most Linear issues have no project. A team backlog view surfaces them. |
+| O4 | `work_items.parent_id UUID NULL`, self-FK | Reverses the earlier "keep it flat." Binding decision #2 (migration target) changes the constraint set: a Linear sub-issue carries assignee, priority and dependencies, and has no lossless landing in a checklist. Lossless import beats structural purity. |
+| O5 | **Depth cap is a mode policy, not a schema constraint** | See §2.5. The move that dissolves the migration-target contradiction. |
+| O6 | `tasks` **survives**, frozen at `title/status/due_date` | See §2.6. |
+| O7 | Single parent FK + cycle prevention on write | OpenProject: *"Can I set multiple parents for one work package? No."* A single parent is what makes a recursive CTE terminate. |
+| O8 | Rollups **always derived, never stored** | OpenProject sums children's progress and *ignores* work manually entered on parents. Stored rollups drift. |
+| O9 | Status **categories** locked in now | Our `phase` enum is already the category layer. Lock the principle immediately: a custom state name always maps to an immutable category. Every burndown, automation and rollup reads the category, never the name. Retrofitting this under modes is misery. |
+| O10 | Client-minted UUIDs on create | Trivial; enables optimistic UI. Linear's Object Pool is keyed by client-known UUID; the human ID is a projection. |
+
+### 2.5 Depth: cap at 1, as policy
+
+- **Schema:** `parent_id` unconstrained. Store `depth` and preserved imported-parent lineage.
+- **Policy:** native creation depth defaults to **1**, a knob in mode config.
+- **Imports** land at **true depth** with `source='import'`, bypassing the cap. The UI renders the
+  real tree.
+
+Why 1:
+
+- **Jira held that line for 20+ years, in writing.** JRASERVER-4446, closed *Won't Do* (2017):
+  *"we should focus on making Jira simpler and easier to use rather then introduce additional
+  complexity."* Premium adds levels only *above* epic. Jira stayed dominant.
+- **Asana ships deep nesting and its own blog begs users not to use it** — *"we recommend not going
+  more than a single 'layer' deep."* Subtasks don't appear by default in Timeline, Calendar, List,
+  Board, Workload or Rules. Users petition Asana to *add* a cap.
+- **The asymmetry is decisive.** ClickUp's docs admit that lowering the cap does not touch existing
+  trees — *"any existing nested subtasks are not impacted."* Lowering is impossible. monday.com
+  *raised* depth from 1 to 4 and could ship it only on **newly created boards**, because the storage
+  *shape* changed. Raising is cheap **if the shape is already right**. So: ship the cap low, ship the
+  shape deep.
+- **We already have a second tier.** work item → sub-item → checklist item is Jira's exact shape.
+
+The strongest argument against, recorded honestly: we are a migration target and Linear is the
+marquee source. It is defused twice — the import bypasses the cap, and **Linear's issue depth is not
+documented anywhere**, so we cannot be "lossy by N" against an unspecified N. (The widely-repeated
+"5 levels" figure applies to Linear *initiatives*, not issues.)
+
+### 2.6 Why `tasks` survives
+
+The teardown recommends collapsing `work_items` + `tasks` into one table with a `type_id` FK, on the
+grounds that *no surveyed product ships a two-table split.* That conflates two different things.
+
+Plane's and OpenProject's single-table lesson is about **work-item types** — bug, feature, epic,
+milestone as *data* rather than an enum. We already honor that with `work_items.type`, and their
+evidence argues for eventually promoting `type` to a table with per-type properties. It does **not**
+argue for merging checklists into issues. Every product cited has **both**: Linear has sub-issues
+*and* markdown checklists; Jira has sub-tasks *and* checklists.
+
+Once `parent_id` exists, the "two-table hierarchy tax" disappears: hierarchy, rollups and relations
+are written once, against `work_items` only. `tasks` joins nothing. Making a five-item checklist mint
+five full work items would pollute boards, counts and identifiers.
+
+**Guard rail + pressure valve.** `tasks` is frozen at `title/status/due_date`. The moment anyone asks
+for an assignee, a priority or a dependency on one, the answer is **promote to sub-item** — a
+one-click action. Build it; it is cheap and it is where the pressure goes.
+
+### 2.6.1 Sub-items have no create button
+
+The founder's challenge — *"why would any person add a sub-task? Make it a checklist instead"* — is
+correct about the **affordance**, and it is upheld.
+
+- **There is no "Add sub-item" action in the default UI.** The everyday way to break work down is the
+  Checklist.
+- **Sub-items are born exactly two ways:** an **import**, or a **promote-from-checklist**.
+- `parent_id` remains in the schema for import fidelity and as promote's landing spot.
+- A mode may later enable direct sub-item creation (Jira-refugee teams expect it). That is a **mode
+  toggle**, not a default.
+
+Keep promote. A Step that grows an assignee or a discussion needs somewhere to go; without the valve,
+users file duplicate Items instead.
+
+### 2.6.2 Status: categories and statuses replace `phase`
+
+The current `phase` enum — `plan | execute | review | done` — carries several jobs and is missing two
+states. `plan` is being used as a backlog; there is no way to say an Item died rather than shipped.
+
+**Migration-target positioning forces the fix:** Linear's **Backlog** and **Canceled** have no lossless
+home in our enum, so import breaks today.
+
+- **Immutable categories** (global, never mode-editable): `backlog | unstarted | started | completed |
+  canceled`, plus reserved `triage`.
+- **Per-team `statuses` table** — `(team_id, name, category, position)`. `work_items` gains `status_id`.
+  Teams customize *names* and order; categories are fixed. Every rollup, burndown and automation reads
+  the **category**, never the name.
+
+Resolving the specific gaps:
+
+| Question | Ruling |
+|---|---|
+| Is "planning" the backlog? | Yes — and **Backlog and Unstarted are two separate categories**, not one with a flag. "Ideas nobody has committed to" ≠ "agreed, not begun." A flag corrupts both the import mapping and the board columns. |
+| Is **Review** a category? | **No — a status inside `started`.** Linear has no Review category; inventing one breaks import. |
+| "Does this need review, or can it be pushed?" | A **mode policy**: the mode declares review-required-before-`completed`. It lives where workflow enforcement already lives. Jira-tight enforces; Notion-loose does not. |
+| Is **Triage** a category or a view over `source`? | **A category** (reserved), so Linear triage imports losslessly; the inbox UI is a category filter. A mode toggle decides whether `source != 'manual'` items land in `triage` or `backlog`. It is **not** merely a view over `source`: **source is provenance, triage is state.** An accepted agent-created Item leaves triage but keeps its source. |
+
+**Guard rail:** categories are globally immutable. Modes own status *sets*, review policy, the triage
+toggle, and the depth cap — but never category semantics. Otherwise import and cross-team reporting
+both die.
+
+### 2.7 Migration
+
+Expand/contract, additive, zero-downtime — as discipline against live traffic, **not** as a driver
+workaround (see §5.1).
+
+**One wave, not three.** `teams`, the status-category refactor, and `parent_id` all touch
+`work_items`. `statuses` depends on `team_id`. Migrate once, and do it **before `phase` data
+accrues** — i.e. before Phase 1 onboarding, never during.
+
+1. `CREATE TABLE teams`
+2. `INSERT INTO teams SELECT DISTINCT department …` (1:1, founder-confirmed)
+3. `ALTER TABLE work_items ADD COLUMN team_id`; backfill via update-join; `SET NOT NULL`
+4. `CREATE TABLE statuses (team_id, name, category, position)`; seed a default set per team
+5. `ALTER TABLE work_items ADD COLUMN status_id`; backfill from `phase`:
+   `plan→backlog`, `execute→started`, `review→started` + an "In Review" status, `done→completed`
+6. `ADD COLUMN parent_id`, `ADD COLUMN depth`
+7. `projects` enrichment: `status`, `lead_id`, `target_date`
+
+`phase` is retained, deprecated, alongside `department` — both dropped in the same future major.
+
+`department` is retained, deprecated, for one full contract cycle — **Forge depends on it**. Dropping
+it is the future major. Contracts take a **minor** semver bump; everything here is additive.
+
+Cycle-safe reparenting is a single `UPDATE` guarded by a `NOT EXISTS` recursive CTE. For the residual
+A→B / B→A race under read-committed, use `sql.transaction()` at Serializable.
+
+### 2.8 Deferred
+
+Cycles (team-scoped when built), initiatives (group projects), project milestones, per-team `ENG-123`
+identifiers, the `/sync/delta` endpoint, the persisted offline transaction queue, Vikunja-style
+auto-inverse relations.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Amendment to the canonical doc, §6
+
+**Current text** puts the mode preset at the Project level with an org default and ceiling.
+
+**Why it is wrong.** Modes bundle required fields, workflow/phase enforcement, cycles on-or-off, task
+structure, who-can-edit-shape, and agent autonomy. In Linear, *Team* owns exactly those. Putting them
+on Project breaks twice:
+
+1. A project may span multiple teams — so a cross-team project would impose one team's workflow on
+   another team's items.
+2. `project_id` is nullable, so items with no project would be **ungoverned**. Making project
+   mandatory to fix this requires fake per-team Inbox projects, which corrupt import semantics.
+
+**Amended text.** *Modes are presets applied at the **TEAM** level, with an org default and an
+optional ceiling. Project is a first-class cross-team outcome container — status, lead, target date,
+milestones — and does not own workflow. Project-level overrides: deferred.*
+
+This also makes the migration-target positioning honest: Linear and Jira imports map team→team,
+workflow-per-team, losslessly.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Release phasing
+
+### 4.1 The capture problem, stated plainly
+
+A browser cannot reliably hear the other participants.
+
+- Per Chrome's own browser-compat-data: `getDisplayMedia({audio:true})` captures entire-system audio
+  **only on Windows/ChromeOS when sharing a whole screen**. On **Linux and macOS it captures tab
+  audio only**. **Firefox: no audio capture. Safari: no. All mobile browsers: no.** And `video:false`
+  throws a `TypeError` — you must carry a video track you discard.
+- **None** of Otter, Fireflies, Fathom, Granola, tl;dv or Read.ai capture from a plain web page. Each
+  uses a bot, a desktop app, or an extension. Granola's docs: *"The web interface … is for viewing and
+  editing existing notes only — it cannot capture or transcribe."*
+- Both credible "no account" open-source precedents **abandoned the browser**: Meetily (MIT) is a
+  Tauri desktop app; Vexa (Apache-2.0) is a self-hosted bot.
+
+And "just upload your recording" does not rescue it. Verified against vendor docs:
+
+| Platform | Free tier records? | Audio-only export |
+|---|---|---|
+| Google Meet | **No** — needs Business Standard+/Enterprise/Edu Plus, or Google One ≥2 TB | No (MP4 only) |
+| Zoom | **Local yes**, cloud needs Pro+ | Yes (`audio_only.m4a`) |
+| Microsoft Teams | **No** — free Teams cannot record at all | No (MP4; Personal-tier expires in 30 days) |
+
+Two of three gate recording behind a paid seat. Upload is a fallback for a paid-tier minority, not an
+onboarding path.
+
+### 4.2 The capture ladder
+
+One `CaptureSource` seam, three tiers, cheapest first:
+
+- **Tier 0 — Upload.** Universal fallback and demo. Zero marginal cost; the pipeline exists.
+- **Tier 1 — Chrome tab capture.** The loophole: macOS gives *tab* audio, and **Google Meet runs in a
+  tab**. "Capture this tab" + mic covers Meet-in-browser on every desktop Chrome platform, with no
+  bot and no desktop app. Ships in weeks. Degrade **honestly**: tell Safari/Firefox/mobile users it
+  cannot work, rather than failing silently.
+- **Tier 2 — Self-hosted bot. The flagship.** **Deploy Vexa (Apache-2.0); do not build a bot.** It is
+  the "no third-party login" promise made literal: open-source, self-hosted, working regardless of the
+  attendee's OS or browser. Pin to a specific commit and stay fork-ready — Screenpipe relicensed from
+  AGPL to proprietary on 2026-06-10.
+
+**Accepted architectural exception:** bots need containers, not Workers. **"All-Cloudflare" governs
+the app plane** (SPA, API, DB). The media-capture plane legitimately lives in containers. Stated
+explicitly rather than pretended away.
+
+### 4.3 STT and diarization
+
+- **Hosted default: Cloudflare Workers AI `whisper-large-v3-turbo`, $0.0306/recording-hour** — ~25×
+  cheaper than Deepgram streaming, already inside our stack, no new vendor.
+- **Self-host default: faster-whisper** — MIT code *and* MIT weights, CPU int8 viable, no account.
+- **BYO-key adapters** for Deepgram / AssemblyAI / Groq / Sarvam when live captions matter. Every one
+  is REST/WS + bearer token.
+- Keep the existing Python `SpeechProvider` seam. Add `supportsStreaming` and `supportsDiarization`
+  capability flags so the UI degrades honestly.
+- **Diarization: do NOT adopt pyannote or WhisperX.** pyannote's weights sit behind a HuggingFace
+  accept-conditions wall plus a marketing opt-in, and **WhisperX inherits that gate** — it silently
+  breaks the no-third-party-login promise. Keep the heuristic for v1; adopt **NVIDIA streaming
+  Sortformer** (CC-BY-4.0, ungated) when diarization matters.
+- In-Worker inference is **impossible**: 10 MB bundle cap, 128 MB memory, 5 min CPU.
+- A Worker **can** hold a long-lived WebSocket for a whole meeting. **One Durable Object per meeting +
+  WebSocket Hibernation** is the natural session object. We do **not** need an SFU — we ship one
+  client's audio to one backend, not media between participants.
+
+### 4.4 Phases
+
+| Phase | Weeks | Ships | Onboards | The one thing that sinks it |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| **1** | 1–4 | Workboard GA in `platform-web`; ontology migration | First users, on the workboard | The migration destabilizing the live app — **migrate before onboarding, not during** |
+| **2** | 3–8 (overlaps) | Meeting surfaces in `platform-web` against the existing Python API; Tier 0 + Tier 1 capture; **timeboxed 2-week Vexa self-host spike, starting week one** | Meeting users | The **capture spike**. Second risk: the Clerk↔Python auth bridge |
+| **3** | 8–16 | Bot GA; proposals spine + first workboard MCP toolset; transcription strangler (Cut 2) | — | The rebuild going big-bang |
+
+If the Vexa spike fails, meeting GA slides into Phase 3 while workboard growth continues. The plan
+degrades gracefully rather than inverting.
+
+**Strangler reorder: transcription moves FIRST, not last**, in two cuts.
+
+- **Cut 1 (days):** add a `workers-ai` provider to the existing Python `SpeechProvider` registry,
+  calling Workers AI over REST. $0.03/hr, no new vendor, no migration.
+- **Cut 2:** move the transcription job (ingest → transcribe → persist) to a Worker + queue. It is the
+  compute-heavy stateless piece — the right first endpoint to strangle.
+
+### 4.5 The agent plane is a trap as scoped
+
+AG-UI + CopilotKit + model router + memory, before any board has an agent, is a six-month hole. The
+minimal increment that genuinely parallelizes:
+
+**the `proposals` table + review UI + ONE MCP toolset over the workboard API.**
+
+Proposals is the spine both boards' agents write through. Build the spine, not the plane. Note that
+`proposals` is currently **prose in `DESIGN.md` only** — no table, no code — yet the canonical doc
+makes it the guard rail for agent-first setup.
+
+### 4.6 `apps/meeting-web`
+
+**Freeze it.** Bugfix-only reference implementation. Build meeting surfaces fresh in `platform-web`
+(they need the design system anyway); delete `meeting-web` at parity. Folding it in now imports
+Python-era UI debt; amending the "one app" decision reverses a FINAL decision for convenience.
+
+### 4.7 The honest v1 pitch
+
+Not "post-meeting intelligence" held hostage to a file users often cannot produce:
+
+> *The open-source meeting notetaker you can self-host — your calls never touch a third party — whose
+> notes become action items on your team's board.*
+
+Interim line until the bot is GA: *"works today for Chrome-attended meetings and any recording you
+have."*
+
+---
+
+## 5. Corrections to previously-held beliefs
+
+### 5.1 "The Neon HTTP driver has no interactive transactions" — FALSE
+
+This was inherited from a code comment (`apps/platform-api/src/routes/dependencies.ts`) and repeated
+into design reasoning. Neon's docs:
+
+- Over **HTTP**: `sql.transaction(queries, {isolationLevel, readOnly, deferrable})` runs multiple
+  statements in one atomic **non-interactive** transaction, with a selectable isolation level.
+- Over **WebSockets**: `Pool`/`Client` give **full interactive transactions**, explicitly supported on
+  Cloudflare Workers. The only constraint is that the Pool/Client is created, used and closed inside a
+  single request handler.
+- Schema migrations run through drizzle-kit against a direct connection, where transactions were
+  always available.
+
+**Impact:** it invalidated the "migrations must be single idempotent statements" rationale, and it was
+the decisive leg of the depth-cap argument (that depth-1 makes a concurrent-move cycle race
+impossible). Neither conclusion changed; both rationales did. **Follow-up: fix the comment.**
+
+### 5.2 "Linear caps sub-issues at 5 levels" — FALSE
+
+The 5-level cap applies to **initiatives**. Linear's sub-issue depth is not documented anywhere.
+
+---
