+## 4. Transport / Architecture on Cloudflare
+
+From https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/limits/ [verified]:
+- **Memory: 128 MB. Worker size: 10 MB. CPU time: max 5 min (default 30s).**
+- **Duration (wall clock): HTTP request = *no limit* while the client stays connected.** Durable Objects (RPC/HTTP) = **unlimited** while the caller stays connected.
+- Cron/Queue/DO-alarm handlers = 15 min.
+
+⇒ **A Worker can hold a long-lived WebSocket for a whole meeting.** [verified]
+⇒ **In-Worker Whisper inference is impossible**: a 10 MB bundle cap and 128 MB memory cannot hold Whisper weights, and 5 min CPU won't cover an hour of audio. Inference must go to Workers AI (separate GPU service) or an external provider. [reasoning, grounded in the limits above]
+
+**Durable Objects + WebSocket Hibernation** is the right primitive: *"Clients remain connected while the Durable Object is not in memory"*, *"Billable Duration (GB-s) charges do not accrue during hibernation"*, and it wakes on message. Restore state via `serializeAttachment`/`deserializeAttachment`. https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/best-practices/websockets/ [verified] One DO per meeting = the natural session/coordination object.
+
+**Cloudflare Realtime** ships **RealtimeKit** (high-level SDK), the **Realtime SFU** (low-level, *"Requires deep WebRTC knowledge. No SDK provided"*), and **TURN**. https://developers.cloudflare.com/realtime/ [verified]
+**LiveKit server is Apache-2.0** and self-hostable. https://github.com/livekit/livekit + [LICENSE](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/livekit/livekit/master/LICENSE) [verified]
+
+**Recommendation:** you do not need an SFU. You are not routing media between participants — you are shipping one client's audio to one backend. **Raw WebSocket → Durable Object** is sufficient and far cheaper in complexity than SFU/WebRTC. Add LiveKit only if you later build a bot that must *join* WebRTC calls. [reasoning]
+
+**Rust/WASM:** useful in exactly two places — (a) **client-side**: resample/VAD/Opus-encode in the browser before upload, and the in-browser engine tier (whisper.cpp WASM, Transformers.js ONNX+WebGPU); (b) **self-host container**: whisper.cpp / faster-whisper. It is **not** useful inside a Worker (bundle + memory caps). [reasoning]
+
+---
+
+## 5. The No-Lock-In Design
+
+The standard pattern is what you already have in FastAPI: a **provider interface + adapters**, with three tiers — (1) a bundled default engine that needs no account, (2) BYO-key adapters for every hosted API, (3) an optional hosted default. Evidence from the field:
+
+- **Meetily** (`Zackriya-Solutions/meeting-minutes`) — **MIT**, Tauri (Rust + Next.js), captures **mic + system audio** at the OS layer, local Whisper/Parakeet + Ollama, fully offline. Rationale: data sovereignty. https://github.com/Zackriya-Solutions/meeting-minutes [verified]
+- **Vexa** — Apache-2.0, bot-based, Whisper self-hostable, air-gap ready. README: *"Every meeting-AI tool… sends your conversations to their cloud and rents you access back. Vexa inverts that."* [verified]
+- **Amurex** — **AGPL-3.0**, browser extension for Meet/Teams web, self-hostable backend. https://github.com/thepersonalaicompany/amurex [verified; capture mechanism not documented → CNV]
+- **Screenpipe** — **license flipped 2026-06-10 to a proprietary source-available "Screenpipe Commercial License"**: free only for personal/non-commercial/eval; commercial use requires payment; hosting-as-a-service prohibited. https://github.com/mediar-ai/screenpipe/blob/main/LICENSE.md [verified]
+
+**The lesson:** Screenpipe is the cautionary tale (AGPL → commercial) and the reason to pick permissive deps deliberately. Meetily (MIT) and Vexa (Apache-2.0) are the credible "no account" precedents — and **both abandoned the browser for capture**: Meetily is a desktop app, Vexa is a bot.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Synthesis
+
+**Capture strategy.** Ship a **three-tier capture ladder** behind one `CaptureSource` seam:
+1. **Tier 0 (web, today):** `getDisplayMedia({video:{displaySurface:"browser"}, audio:true, systemAudio:"exclude", selfBrowserSurface:"exclude"})` + `getUserMedia` mic, mixed via WebAudio. Works on Chrome/Edge desktop for tab-based meetings. Detect and *tell the user* on Safari/Firefox/mobile rather than failing silently. Discard the forced video track.
+2. **Tier 1 (self-host parity):** a **bot** (Vexa/Attendee-style headless Chrome) for users who want all platforms and no per-session picker.
+3. **Tier 2 (hosted convenience):** Recall.ai at $0.50/hr, or a desktop helper.
+
+Do not pretend Tier 0 is sufficient. It is a Chrome-desktop, tab-meeting feature — and on macOS it cannot hear system audio at all.
+
+**Default self-hosted STT:** **faster-whisper** (MIT code + MIT weights, viable CPU int8, drop-in) with **whisper.cpp** for the CPU/edge and WASM build. **Default hosted:** **Cloudflare Workers AI whisper-large-v3-turbo** at **$0.0306/hr** — same stack, no new vendor — with **Deepgram/AssemblyAI as BYO-key streaming adapters** when live captions matter. Keep the existing `SpeechProvider` seam; add `supportsStreaming` and `supportsDiarization` capability flags so the UI degrades honestly.
+
+**Diarization default:** streaming **Sortformer** (CC-BY-4.0, ungated). Avoid pyannote in the default path — its HF gate breaks "no proprietary account."
+
+### Top 5 decisions by impact/cost
+1. **Accept that "web-first" ≠ "browser-captures-everything"** — ship the capture ladder, not one mechanism. (Highest impact, zero code cost: it's a scoping decision.)
+2. **One Durable Object per meeting, WebSocket Hibernation** — long-lived session, no idle billing, unlimited wall time. Cheap, exactly fits.
+3. **Workers AI as hosted default** ($0.0306/hr) — 25× cheaper than Deepgram streaming, no new vendor.
+4. **faster-whisper as the bundled self-host engine** — MIT top-to-bottom, CPU-viable, no account.
+5. **Sortformer over pyannote** for diarization — the only choice consistent with the no-account promise.
+
+### Top 3 traps
+1. **macOS has no system audio in the browser, ever.** Chrome's own compat note says Linux/macOS get *tab audio only*. Any roadmap assuming otherwise is dead on arrival for the majority of notetaker users.
+2. **pyannote's HuggingFace gate** silently contaminates "fully open" — WhisperX inherits it. You'd ship a self-host story that demands a HF account and a terms click.
+3. **License drift in your dependencies.** Screenpipe went proprietary on 2026-06-10; Moonshine's non-English weights are non-commercial. Pin licenses and re-check at each bump.
+
+---
